---

#dash.flags
  Most urgent first: Festgefahren (werkrevisor escalated, RED for an hour
  without a new commit, ERROR, or a run without verdict), Dein Approval (a
  foreign pull request with a final ORANGE or YELLOW, or without werkrevisor),
  Mergen (the user's own pull request approved by another human, or a foreign
  one approved by anyone but its author, all checks green, no automerge),
  Nacharbeit (conflict or red build on the user's own pull request), Wartet auf
  Review (the user's own pull request waits for another human), Von dir
  approved. Only the chips are coloured, not the row.

#dash.pwa
  User request of 2026-10-10: the Werkregie frontend becomes a progressive
  web app, installable on phone and desktop.

  User decision of 2026-10-10: the last state, with pull request and session
  data, may be kept in the browser's storage for the offline view.

  The service worker caches the page, styles, fonts and icons only. The API
  answers carry private data behind the access token and are never cached by
  the service worker; offline the page shows the last state it holds, marked
  with its age. The local mode on localhost counts as a
  secure context, so the PWA works there too.

#dash.matrix
  User request of 2026-10-10: messages that matter to the user show in
  Werkregie, read-only. Werkregie signs in with the user's own account as a
  device of its own, because many rooms are end-to-end encrypted and only a
  device of the account can read them. It runs in the relay, so the device
  keys stay on the user's machine.

  The homeserver is configured; there is no default, and no real homeserver
  appears in the repository. Shown: every direct message, mentions in every
  room (found after decryption, since the server cannot see them in encrypted
  rooms), and all messages of the configured rooms. The server gets only room,
  sender, time and the start of the text.

  Reading Element Desktop's own store was dropped: it has no interface, and
  its data lies encrypted in IndexedDB.

#dash.matrix.sdk
  Checked with Trixnity 5.9.2 on 2026-10-10, without signing in anywhere.
  It compiles with the dashboard's Kotlin 2.2.21 on Temurin 21 and pulls
  kotlin-stdlib 2.3.21. vodozemac comes through JNI: the jar carries the
  native library for Linux x86-64 and aarch64 and unpacks it into the temp
  directory, which must allow execution; it needs glibc 2.34 (mih09 has
  2.36). Olm and Megolm round trips work, and so do discovery and versions
  against matrix.org and the user's homeserver.

  For the bundle: 49 jars and 24 MB more, and jlink needs java.logging,
  java.management, java.instrument and jdk.unsupported besides the
  modules it has. The client's store comes next:
  trixnity-client-repository-exposed on the sqlite-jdbc the dashboard has.

#dash.taiga
  User request of 2026-10-11: plan a Taiga integration. Its scope is open:
  which user stories and tasks show, the user's own or a project's; whether
  a story links to its pull requests and its Werkbaum node, which marks a
  subtree with &taiga.<project-slug>; and which Taiga instances and tokens.

#dash.matrix.accounts
  matrix becomes a list of named accounts, as giteas; a single block is still
  read. Each account gets its own directory matrix/<name>/ with store, lock
  and messages.json, a source matrix:<name>, and matrix login|verify <name>.

#dash.matrix.link
  Element Desktop opens element:// links (io.element.desktop:// for the
  stable app when a nightly build holds element://); on a phone matrix.to
  opens Element. PR#8 makes it configurable as matrix.link, matrix.to by
  default.

#dash.store
  The Matrix client needs a store; Trixnity's Exposed repositories need
  R2DBC, which SQLite does not offer, so PR#8 uses an H2 file for it.
  sqlite-jdbc stays as long as Werkregie reads Codex: it only reads Codex'
  own database. Whether Werkregie gets one database of its own, H2 or
  another, and what moves into it, is to be discussed.

#dash.notify
  Werkregie posts what a pull request asks of whom into a Matrix room, for
  colleagues too. A separate feature, later. A bot account of its own will
  do; which events go to whom is open.

#ops.github
  GitHub carries main only. Until Werkator does it, main is pushed by hand
  with git push github.

#ops.port
  The server runs on port 18091, picked because it was free; Hostsharing has
  not confirmed it for the booked service yet.
